data.body:
No data is required to play: guest mode asks for no name, no email, no registration.
Depending on how you use the app, the following categories of data may be processed:
· pseudonymous technical identifier: randomly generated and stored in a cookie, used to recognize your browser from one visit to the next (resuming a game, one game per day, win streak). Purpose: providing the game. Legal basis: performance of the requested service (Article 6.1.b GDPR);
· connected devices: for each browser you play on, the app keeps the date of first login, the date of the last visit and a rough label of the browser and device (for example "Safari · iPhone", never the full technical header). With an account, the list of your devices is shown in your profile, where you can log out each one. Purpose: account security and cross-device login. Legal basis: performance of the requested service (Article 6.1.b);
· email and pseudo, only if you create an account: an optional account, activated via a login link sent by email (no password), lets you find your history and streak again on any device; the pseudo is a display name you choose freely. Purpose: account management and cross-device login. Legal basis: your consent (Article 6.1.a GDPR), you remain free to play as a guest without ever providing this information;
· game data: the result of each answer (whether the artist and title were found, without the text you type, which is never kept), chosen listening durations, scores and play dates. Purpose: running the game and computing anonymous global statistics (median score, success rate per track). Legal basis: performance of the requested service (Article 6.1.b). With an account, the publisher can view in the administration area your username, email address, streak and the history of your games over the last 30 days, solely to run the service and answer your requests;
· Platines mode (invitation only): for each game, the result of each track (whether the artist and title were found, never the text you type), its points, the rounds passed, the score and the dates played; your record (best score and best disc); the playlists you launch and their author; the access the publisher granted you. A playlist is shared through a link: whoever opens it sees its author's pseudo ("Guest" without an account) and, once their own game is over, the pseudo (or "Guest"), disc and score of everyone who played it. This is not a public leaderboard: only people with the link can see them. If you delete your account, your games are deleted; the playlists you launched stay playable, without an author. Purpose: running this mode and the comparison between people who played the same playlist. Legal basis: performance of the requested service (Article 6.1.b);
· IP address: technically processed on every request, as with any website, for security purposes (limiting the number of login attempts to prevent abuse). Purpose: service security. Legal basis: the controller's legitimate interest (Article 6.1.f);
· technical logs: for each request, the application records the pseudonymous technical identifier described above, the page or action involved, its duration, its outcome, the browser and device type (for example "Safari · iPhone") and, when a bug occurs, the error message, its stack trace, the app version and the connection status (online or not). These logs never contain your email, your pseudo, your submitted answers or your cookie. Purpose: detecting and fixing bugs, monitoring that the service runs properly. Legal basis: the controller's legitimate interest (Article 6.1.f);
· audience measurement: on each page you view, a small Cloudflare Web Analytics script sends Cloudflare the page address, the site you came from, your browser and device type, and the page load times. Cloudflare turns this into aggregate statistics (visits, page views, countries, referring sites, devices, site speed), without any cookie or identifier that could recognise you; the publisher only sees these statistics. Purpose: knowing how much the site is visited and how fast it is. Legal basis: the controller's legitimate interest (Article 6(1)(f));
· arrival source: when your guest identifier is created, the app records where you came from: the campaign name carried by the link you followed (for example "instagram", or "share" for a link shared by a player), otherwise only the domain name of the site you came from (for example "google.com", never the full page address), or "direct". It is kept in your browser for the duration of the visit, then attached to your pseudonymous identifier; it is included in the copy of your data and deleted with it. Purpose: knowing which channels bring new players (audience measurement). Legal basis: the controller's legitimate interest (Article 6(1)(f));
· local preferences: your preferred language and a record of the information messages already shown are stored locally in your browser; they never leave your device and are never sent to our servers.

thirdParties.body:
Your data is never sold, transferred or shared with third parties for commercial or advertising purposes. It is only shared with the technical providers strictly necessary to run the service:
· hosting: the application, its code and its database are hosted on a private server (VPS) provided by OVH SAS (see the legal notice), in a data center located in Gravelines (France);
· network and domain name: the domain name inseconds.cc runs through Cloudflare, Inc.'s network (United States), which acts as a technical relay (DNS resolution, DDoS and abuse protection): your exchanges with the site pass through it, but Cloudflare does not use them for its own purposes; it also produces for the publisher the traffic statistics described in "Data collected" (Cloudflare Web Analytics);
· backups: database backup copies are encrypted on our server before being sent to Cloudflare, Inc.'s R2 storage service, in its European jurisdiction (data stored in the European Union); Cloudflare does not have the encryption key and cannot read them;
· sending emails: if you create an account, the email containing your login link (or the link confirming an address change) is sent via the Brevo API (Sendinblue SAS, France, data hosted in the European Union); only your email address is shared with them, solely to deliver that message;
· technical logs: the logs described above are stored by Grafana Labs, in the European region of its Grafana Cloud service, so the publisher can look them up when a bug occurs;
· monitoring of automated tasks: the healthchecks.io service receives a simple signal at the end of each nightly task (daily challenge generation, backup…) and alerts the publisher if one of them fails; no data about you is sent to it;
· music excerpts and covers: provided by Deezer (Deezer S.A., France). Track search goes through our server, but your browser loads the excerpts and covers directly from Deezer's servers, which therefore receive your IP address, as with any resource loaded by a web page. No other information about you (account, username, email, games) is sent to Deezer (see "Music content" in the legal notice).
Transfers outside the European Union: Cloudflare and Grafana Labs, based in the United States, may process data outside the EU. These transfers are governed by the European Commission's standard contractual clauses, built into their respective data processing agreements (DPAs), as well as by their adherence to the EU-U.S. Data Privacy Framework, two mechanisms recognized by the European Commission as offering protection safeguards equivalent to the GDPR.

security.body:
InSeconds implements reasonable technical measures to protect your data:
· all communication between your browser and our servers is encrypted (HTTPS);
· the identification cookie is encrypted and marked HttpOnly (inaccessible to a script running on the page, including in the event of an XSS flaw);
· logging in by email relies on no password: only a single-use link, valid for 15 minutes, is used, and only its cryptographic fingerprint is stored in the database, never the link itself;
· login and account creation attempts are rate-limited per IP address, to prevent automated abuse;
· an email address change only takes effect once confirmed through a link sent to the new address;
· each device connected to your account can be logged out from your profile, one by one or all at once, for example if you lose a phone;
· database backups are encrypted before leaving the server;
· the admin area is only accessible to accounts that the publisher has manually granted this role: it uses the same email-link login as players, with no additional password, and no feature of the application allows anyone to obtain this role.
No security measure offers an absolute guarantee; should a security incident affect your data, we are committed to notifying the CNIL and, where necessary, the individuals concerned, in accordance with Articles 33 and 34 of the GDPR.
